# Production review of the split extraction prototype

**The split is not ready for production.** The most concrete problem is that the same article passage can enter the library twice: in the final first-100 run, sequence 1207 produced two records from the same sentence, and both were written. The new path also lacks a production recovery contract and has not been accepted on the frozen ruler. [Trace, sequence 1207](docs/research/2026-11-05-production-plan/traces/split-v2-final-first100-2026-11-05/1207.json), [writer](src/hivemind/views/writing.py:222), [frozen judgement scorer](docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/score_judgement.py:36).

**Review boundary:** This is a read-only review of the requested path. I inspected the code and examples cited below and counted duplicate passages in the saved final first-100 traces. I did **not** complete a line-by-line audit of every requested file or a semantic review of every saved answer. The tables below therefore identify the production contract that still needs to be completed and tested; they are not a claim that an exhaustive independent review is finished. No files were edited and no model calls or tests were run.

Severity means: **blocker** prevents a responsible production acceptance; **serious** can produce materially wrong or incomplete research; **minor** affects traceability or operational clarity without itself establishing wrong research.

## 1. Minimal production architecture

### Recommended shape

| Piece | Production home and responsibility | Evidence and status |
|---|---|---|
| Per-item context | One library reader obtains **all open questions committed before the item’s sequence**. It supplies the same immutable snapshot to A, B, the link checks and matching. | **Blocker:** the prototype does this at [split runner](docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split_seven_day_poc.py:361), but production still calls the day-start, subject-filtered reader at [extraction](src/hivemind/extraction.py:42). D375 and the later all-open decision require the former behavior: [decision log](docs/DECISIONS-log.md:4031). |
| A, B and preparation | Put request construction, schemas, source checks and handover building in `src/hivemind`, with separately versioned prompt files. A proposes questions with passages; B files passages under the shared snapshot; preparation supplies supported subject, dates, attribution and optional intervals for A’s output. | The prototype assembles these in [item_requests](docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split_seven_day_poc.py:31), [metadata_request](docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split_seven_day_poc.py:136), and [build_handover](docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split_seven_day_poc.py:256). |
| Matching and writing | Feed the checked handover into the existing matching and real writer, including the analyst review for ambiguous unkeyed questions. Preserve the frozen path for old configurations. | [Round](src/hivemind/views/round.py:128), [Jev review](src/hivemind/views/jev_matching.py:345), [writer](src/hivemind/views/writing.py:112). The prototype already invokes the real round at [runner line 533](docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split_seven_day_poc.py:533). |
| Daily judging | After all items for the day finish, judge changed open questions with usable intervals, then run end-of-day processing. A question without intervals remains available for future evidence. | [Daily round](src/hivemind/views/round.py:73), [judging eligibility](src/hivemind/views/judging.py:301), and the [incomplete-details decision](docs/DECISIONS-log.md:4027). |
| Configuration | A **new, explicitly named split version** should identify its A, B, preparation and collision settings, including model, prompt/schema version, reasoning and output limits. It should select the implementation explicitly; it must not infer it from an environment variable. Keep worker limits as run arguments, not configuration. | Current config has one extraction setting per category and no split setting: [config](src/hivemind/config.py:52), [config PRD](docs/prd/03-config.md:22). D359 requires new versions rather than moving frozen ones: [decision](docs/DECISIONS-log.md:3798). |
| Entry points | Replay should load that configuration and call the shared implementation. The current HTTP/MCP service is primarily a **reader of a ledger**; I found no production ingestion entry point there to select the split. An eventual ingestion service must call the same coordinator; do not claim that one exists today. | [Replay entry](scripts/replay.py:77), [HTTP service entry](scripts/hivemind_api.py:1), [MCP entry](scripts/hivemind_mcp.py:1). |

### What happens to the research runner’s special parts

- **Research subjects — serious.** `poc_subjects` is an experiment-local registry, restored into an in-memory universe on restart; it is not durable production subject identity. [Runner](docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split_seven_day_poc.py:319). Production needs a ledger-owned identity and recovery contract for research subjects, including aliases, kind, and collision handling. A text hash alone proves identical spelling, not that two names describe the same subject. It must not turn a list of subjects into a list of what matters.
- **Earlier-run reuse.** Keep reuse in the model archive only when the **entire actual request**, configuration, source, snapshot and response are verified. The research runner’s hard-coded directory and copied files are diagnostic machinery, not a production dependency. [Runner](docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split_seven_day_poc.py:400), [reuse](docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split_seven_day_poc.py:457).
- **Manifest pinning — blocker.** Preserve the purpose of pinning source, runtime, configuration and ordered inputs for an unfinished run, but make it a production run contract rather than hashes of research scripts. The existing parallel runner already refuses changed inputs and runtime; it does not yet pin and validate both split requests and their shared snapshot. [Parallel manifest](src/hivemind/parallel_day.py:68).
- **Cut by limit — blocker if misrepresented as a complete day.** The research runner explicitly stops before daily judging when a limit cuts a day. [Runner](docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split_seven_day_poc.py:540). Production replay’s `max_items_per_day` currently proceeds to judging and marks the day complete after its selected items. [Replay](src/hivemind/replay.py:75), [completion](src/hivemind/replay.py:119). A limited diagnostic must remain visibly incomplete unless its intended scope is explicitly defined as a complete run.
- **Legacy branches.** Old metadata schemas, free-text subject recovery and old-run file layouts are needed to inspect historical results, not as silent fallbacks for new production calls. Keep a clearly versioned historical adapter if required; new calls should have one declared contract. [Legacy preparation](docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split_seven_day_poc.py:86), [subject fallback](docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split_seven_day_poc.py:230).
- **Reader limits.** Main’s D376 and D377 remove the evidence and question-catalog size rejections; they do not authorize a new arbitrary cap on the all-open snapshot. This review did not independently verify their implementation. [D376–D377](</Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:4041>).

## 2. Latest questions and throughput

D375 requires item *n* to see writes from earlier sequences, including earlier items that day. A and B may run together, but both must use the same snapshot. [D375](docs/DECISIONS-log.md:4031).

| Option | Correctness and cost | Recommendation |
|---|---|---|
| **Sequential items, A and B concurrent within each item** | Straightforward ordering and recovery. The draft reports **about 20 seconds per item**; it does not show a timing distribution or separate matching and judging latency. Treat that as a planning estimate, not a verified service-level result. [Plan](docs/research/2026-11-05-production-plan/plan-v0.md:31). | Build as the reference implementation and correctness control. |
| **Speculative preparation of later items** | Prepare A and B ahead; at each turn rebuild both requests from the committed snapshot and accept them only if both match exactly. A changed snapshot invalidates affected work; already-running calls may still be charged. Preparation depends on A and must also be tied to the accepted A answer and subject snapshot. Existing parallel code validates one old extraction request, not this bundle. [Parallel runner](src/hivemind/parallel_day.py:100). | Preferred throughput option **only after** a deterministic replay shows equality with the sequential control and measures discarded calls. |
| **Concurrent independent scopes** | Could be useful only if neither scope reads or writes shared questions or subjects. The all-open rule means that independence cannot be assumed merely because two incoming articles have different company tags. | Do not use until independence is demonstrated by the actual read/write sets. |

D183’s earlier single-call system was about four times faster on a busy day at about **24% more cost**; that is historical evidence for the old workload, not a measurement of split all-open speculation. [D183](docs/DECISIONS-log.md:2456). The split’s first-100 budget estimated A at $0.12, B at $0.13, preparation at $0.03 and matching plus two judging days at about $0.35. [Predictions](docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split-v2-predictions.md:31). Five all-open requests added an estimated **4,954–24,562 text tokens**; that is a context-size estimate, not an observed production cost increase. [All-open study](docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/all-open-context-design.md:20).

**Cheapest next measurement:** replay the stored ordered items with recorded request durations and question changes, compare a sequential schedule with bounded speculation, and report wall time, sent and discarded A/B/preparation calls, tokens and cost. A simulator estimates scheduling; a later controlled live run would still be needed to establish provider latency and actual cost.

## 3. One item: state × event

This is the **recommended contract**, not a description of behavior already implemented. Let **D** be the item’s original publication day, **S** its dispatch sequence, and **E** the day being completed. Restarting on a later computer date does not change D or E. A statement’s own date is kept separately and may be unknown.

| State | Success: what is read and written | Refusal, crash, stop or later restart |
|---|---|---|
| **Before dispatch** | Read the item and classification; assign S in arrival order. Write the dispatch record with D. [Dispatch](src/hivemind/dispatch.py:23). | Incomplete input is returned with its reason. A corrected version needs its own input identity; the current dispatcher explicitly does not support `supersedes` corrections. [Dispatch](src/hivemind/dispatch.py:38). |
| **Snapshot selected** | Read all questions open after sequences `< S`, including earlier writes on D. Persist or reproducibly identify the snapshot, source, configuration and request identities. | If another writer can commit between snapshot and item commit, a single ordered coordinator or an equivalent transactional ordering check is required. A stale snapshot cannot be silently accepted. This concurrency contract is **not proven** by the prototype’s single experiment lock. [Runner lock](docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split_seven_day_poc.py:419). |
| **A and B in flight** | Both read D’s item and the same snapshot. Archive each exact request and response independently. Neither result alone writes research records. | A refusal or transport failure leaves the item unfinished; preserve the successful response and retry only under the same verified request. An incomplete or potentially in-flight response must be inspected, as the research runner requires. [Completed-answer check](docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split_seven_day_poc.py:400). No later item may commit ahead of S. |
| **Preparation** | If A proposed questions, read the accepted A answer, source and subject snapshot; prepare one result for every candidate and passage. If A proposed none, no preparation call is needed. | A refused or incomplete preparation must not produce a partial handover. Retry against the same A answer and request. A changed A answer requires a new preparation request. [Runner](docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split_seven_day_poc.py:474). |
| **Checked handover** | Validate source passages, IDs, links and applicable admission rules; record drops with reasons. Save the complete handover with S and D in the same commit as the successful extraction marker. | On restart use the saved handover only when its original snapshot and configuration remain valid. A changed configuration or corrected source cannot be silently substituted into a partially completed item; preserve the old attempt and start a separately identified attempt under an explicit policy. Existing round saves the handover and marker together. [Round](src/hivemind/views/round.py:105). |
| **Matching** | Read the saved handover and the library as of S’s turn. Record proposed matches, review answers and refusals; use the item’s D for arrival context and the stated original day, when known, for statement ordering. | A refusal stops the item; no later item commits. Current Jev matching has one attempt in `_match`; the alternative matcher has a retry. Recovery must not claim an automatic Jev retry that does not exist. [Round](src/hivemind/views/round.py:35). |
| **Writing** | Atomically write questions, evidence, links, keys and the completion markers for S. Each source passage that represents one stored piece is written once even when linked to more than one question. | On error, roll back the item’s writes and keep the failure record. The writer uses a savepoint and the caller commits its markers; the new subject registry must participate in the same durable boundary. [Writer](src/hivemind/views/writing.py:112), [round](src/hivemind/views/round.py:153). |
| **Day’s judging** | Once every intended item for E has completed, read the questions changed on E and the ledger at the final sequence for E. Record judgements under E, not the restart’s calendar date; then perform E’s end-of-day work. [Round](src/hivemind/views/round.py:64). | A refusal leaves E resumable; successful already-recorded judgements must not duplicate. A stopped or limit-cut day must not be marked complete. A later restart resumes E before advancing to a later day. [Replay](src/hivemind/replay.py:55). |

**Blocker:** the table still needs a design review against actual archive transactions, partial writes, two competing writers, configuration changes and corrected inputs. The prototype’s per-folder files and status JSON are not by themselves a production transaction protocol.

## 4. New values and their readers

| Value | Writer → readers | Gap |
|---|---|---|
| `closest_existing_question`, `what_separates_it` | A returns both; the handover retains only `closest_existing`; Jev’s unkeyed collision review uses it to add an open peer. [Schema](docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split_seven_day_poc.py:71), [handover](docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split_seven_day_poc.py:299), [reader](src/hivemind/views/jev_matching.py:223). | **Serious:** the explanation is not preserved in the handover. If it is required for audit or review, specify where it is stored and who reads it; do not assume the matcher uses it. |
| Research-subject ID, name, kind | Preparation chooses a listed subject or `new`; registry assigns `research:` ID; the in-memory universe, matching, writer and later requests read it. [Runner](docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split_seven_day_poc.py:131), [registry](docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split_seven_day_poc.py:319). | **Serious:** durable identity, alias resolution, competing creation and restart must be specified. Prototype entries have empty watched categories, so simply adding them to `Universe` does not make them equivalent to configured watched subjects. [Universe](src/hivemind/universe.py:46). |
| Shared-quantity `measure`, `opens_as`, scope, period, basis, unit, event type, key version | Preparation emits a proposed type and year; `shared_key` derives the key; matching and writer read it; `view_keys` stores it for later identity checks. [Prototype](docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split_seven_day_poc.py:247), [matching](src/hivemind/views/jev_matching.py:119), [writer](src/hivemind/views/writing.py:37). | **Serious:** the prototype permits only `spend_total`, `capacity`, and `market_size` for this key. D197 says code owns identity for the specified shared quantity; it does not justify treating those three as a complete list of important quantities. Unkeyed cases remain with analyst review; measure the omissions and false merges before changing the contract. [D197](docs/DECISIONS-log.md:2551). |
| Subject, kind, year, due day, date basis and supporting words | Preparation → handover → matching/writer; year and subject affect identity; due day affects end-of-day closure. Basis and supporting words are embedded in `looks_at` when a date survives rather than stored as separate structured fields. [Handover](docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split_seven_day_poc.py:280), [pull](src/hivemind/pull.py:32). | **Serious:** an exact date must be supported, while an unknown date must remain unknown. The current representation makes date provenance harder to query independently. Do not restore a mandatory date gate: the later decision permits missing details. |
| Intervals and bounds | Preparation → `check_ladder`/`check_bounds` → writer’s `rungs` → daily judge and pull. [Prototype](docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split_seven_day_poc.py:287), [judge](src/hivemind/views/judging.py:301). | **Serious:** structural validity does not establish that the reference is the right quantity. The design claims a wrong-base example, but I did not independently locate and verify that exact saved answer; it remains a lead, not a confirmed finding in this review. [Plan](docs/research/2026-11-05-production-plan/plan-v0.md:51). |
| Speaker, original speaker, original day, tier, event time | Preparation for A and B’s own answer → handover → evidence/source records → matching, revision and judging. [Prototype](docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split_seven_day_poc.py:268), [writer](src/hivemind/views/writing.py:28). | **Serious:** source-quote validation proves the quoted words exist, not that summary, speaker, date or tier are correct. Those need source-to-output review. |
| Evidence IDs, links, dropped reasons and group references | Handover → matching → writer → daily changed-question set and judge. [Round](src/hivemind/views/round.py:119). | **Blocker:** the prototype sets `group_refs: []` and supplies no new groups, while the extraction PRD requires group associations and declarations where applicable. This is a contract gap; I have not measured its effect on the saved run. [Prototype](docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split_seven_day_poc.py:297), [PRD](docs/prd/05-extraction.md:29). |

## 5. New path against old checks

| Existing check or rule | What the split does | Severity |
|---|---|---|
| **Source quote and shown IDs** | It checks source text and rejects B links outside the shown IDs. It uses its own `source_quote`, rather than the complete production `sift` path. [Prototype](docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split_seven_day_poc.py:161). | **Serious:** compare both checks on all stored answers, including changed numbers and conditions; a passing quote check is not a semantic check. |
| **One passage stored once** | A and B are appended independently; the production `sift` merges identical quotes and their links, but the split handover does not. [Sift](src/hivemind/extraction.py:682), [split handover](docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split_seven_day_poc.py:267). | **Blocker.** I counted **8 exact duplicate-quote groups in the final first-100 handovers**, without judging whether every repeated passage expresses one or multiple matters. At sequence 1207, the sentence beginning “Nvidia … forecasting roughly 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028” appears as `e1 → c1` and `e2 → v2`; the saved ledger contains both `e4205` and `e4206` at that sequence. This breaks the one-piece/multiple-links requirement. [Trace](docs/research/2026-11-05-production-plan/traces/split-v2-final-first100-2026-11-05/1207.json), [PRD](docs/prd/06-views.md:202). The downstream effect on conclusions has **not** been measured. |
| **Candidate retains evidence** | A requires a passage and the handover drops a candidate if none survives source checking. [Schema](docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split_seven_day_poc.py:71), [handover](docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split_seven_day_poc.py:275). | **Serious:** re-run the equivalent of `candidates_with_evidence` after all filing and matching transformations, including merged passages. The old path also has a personal-memo exception; whether the split covers that input category must be specified rather than assumed. [Old check](src/hivemind/extraction.py:595). |
| **Narrowest home** | B checks that IDs were shown, but the split does not call `file_reading`’s parent-based narrowing. [Prototype](docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split_seven_day_poc.py:306), [old path](src/hivemind/extraction.py:468). | **Serious:** a valid ID is not proof that the passage went to the right question. Preserve the decided narrowest-home behavior and inspect its effects on stored links. |
| **Quarter and year limits** | Old `sift` applies quarter and year checks to candidates. The split applies its year check only when it has a numeric ladder; an otherwise identical question with no ladder bypasses it. [Old path](src/hivemind/extraction.py:619), [split](docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split_seven_day_poc.py:287). | **Serious:** absence of a ladder is allowed, but does not itself settle whether a known period is outside the applicable rule. Re-derive the check for optional fields and the binding-obligation exception; do not simply restore the old mandatory-ladder gate. |
| **Due day and admission** | The split clears unsupported or invalid dates rather than rejecting the question. This agrees with the later incomplete-details decision, but it does not run the old future-date and binding-obligation checks. [Prototype](docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split_seven_day_poc.py:280), [old checks](src/hivemind/extraction.py:523). | **Serious:** re-derive what applies when a date is present, unknown or an estimate. Do not make missing dates grounds for rejection. |
| **Preflight** | Production preflight selects old extraction archive records and replays `sift`; it does not reconstruct and check the split’s A, B and preparation bundle. [Preflight](scripts/preflight.py:106). The saved split adapter result says it replayed 89 answers, 49 candidates and 604 retained evidence items; it explicitly does not establish new-prompt or live-model behavior. [Result](docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/preflight-split-results.json:1). | **Blocker:** add a production preflight that identifies every split request and response, source, snapshot, schema and handover, reports skipped cases and fails on an unexercised check. |
| **Frozen tests** | They pin old prompt files, schemas and matcher/judge request shapes. They do not prove split requests, recovery or quality. [Extraction freeze](tests/test_frozen_extraction_prompt.py:13), [matching/judging freeze](tests/test_frozen_questions_and_judgement.py:14). | **Blocker for acceptance:** retain those tests and add version-specific checks; a green old freeze test is not a split test. |
| **Analyst review of matches** | Unkeyed candidates include the named closest question and Jev’s same/part/other-period proposals at any probability. [Jev](src/hivemind/views/jev_matching.py:220). | **Serious:** review still depends on what A names and Jev proposes. The saved probe reports 2/3 merges for two ambiguous pairs, not deterministic semantic correctness. [Design](docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split-v2-design.md:47). |

## 6. Acceptance on the frozen ruler

**The current frozen judgement scorer cannot directly accept a clean, unseeded run.** It requires `v1` through `v22` to contain the exact frozen seed questions and refuses otherwise. [Scorer](docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/score_judgement.py:36). D370 removed those questions as the active starting library; it preserved the historical seeds and gold. [D370](docs/DECISIONS-log.md:3998).

I recommend asking Alex to decide whether to authorize **both** of these clearly labelled measurements:

1. A **historical compatibility measurement** using the frozen seed library solely as the measurement fixture, with the existing scorer unchanged. It can compare paths under that same starting context, but cannot demonstrate how production starts from an empty library.
2. A **production-shaped, unseeded end-to-end run**, scored for what can be measured without pretending the 22 questions existed. Its question mapping, missing questions, missing judgements and evaluation rules must be designed and reviewed before the run. It cannot be represented as the existing judgement scorer’s result unless that scorer’s requirements are actually met.

For evidence on golden-v5, Alex has already decided the aggregation: **core plus supporting = helpful; not-kept = unhelpful; optional = neutral**, with one research matter per article. No gold file needs editing. D373 says continue scoring and disclose unresolved concerns; it does not resolve the disputed question entries. [D372–D373](docs/DECISIONS-log.md:4012).

Acceptance must also inspect the source beside missed helpful evidence and examples of retained evidence, attribution, dates, uncertainty and delivery to the question and daily judgement. The frozen extraction count alone does not establish correct filing. [Filing PRD](docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md:25). The first-100 experiment used a 68-question historical starting ledger and was cut partway through a day; it is useful diagnostic evidence, not that acceptance. [Predictions](docs/research/2026-11-05-seven-day-request/split-v2-predictions.md:5).

**Decision still needed from Alex:** the measurement arrangement above and the final production promotion. The split experiment and all-open context have authorization as experimental work; the records do not establish that this split configuration has been accepted as the production default. [Plan](docs/research/2026-11-05-production-plan/plan-v0.md:23), [all-open decision](docs/DECISIONS-log.md:4045).

## 7. Ordered work plan

| Order | Work | Cheapest proof and one accepting test |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | **Resolve the handover contract**, including one stored passage with its valid links, group associations, optional fields and the old checks that still apply. Do not add company-specific rules. | Replay the saved handovers on copies; sequence 1207 must produce one piece with the intended links. **Accepting test:** real writer on a temporary ledger asserts one piece and both appropriate destinations; a mutation that removes the merge fails. |
| 2 | **Make the per-item snapshot and recovery contract explicit**, including two writers, stopped work, changed config and corrected input. | Replay two saved ordered items where the first creates a question the second sees. **Accepting test:** crash and restart between stages produces the same final ledger as uninterrupted serial processing, with A and B sharing the second item’s snapshot. |
| 3 | **Build the versioned split path and durable subject identity** in the shared production modules, retaining the frozen path. | Replay stored A/B/preparation answers through real checks and writer at no cost. **Accepting test:** after restart, a previously created research subject resolves to the same ID and is visible to the next item’s A, B and matcher. |
| 4 | **Extend preflight and frozen-version checks.** | Reconstruct stored requests and compare their actual wire representation and snapshots; report every skipped case. **Accepting test:** an altered source, snapshot or preparation answer is refused, and an empty split population is reported unexercised. |
| 5 | **Measure throughput before choosing speculative concurrency.** | Simulate the recorded ordered calls and snapshot changes. **Accepting test:** bounded speculation produces the same accepted requests and ledger as serial processing while reporting all stale calls and their estimated cost. |
| 6 | **Complete the source/output review of quality gaps**, particularly wrong or missing filing, question opening, subject and interval references. | Read each affected saved source and answer, classify the full observed class, and trace it to the request or code before proposing a change. **Accepting test:** the chosen general change flips the decisive stored case and its effect on the counted class is reported; no benchmark-specific list is introduced. |
| 7 | **Agree the acceptance measurement with Alex**, then run the approved final configuration once under that plan. | First prove that each scorer consumes the intended ledger and frozen inputs, and show what it refuses. **Accepting test:** the registered end-to-end measurement meets its prewritten criteria, with every draw, regression, missing question and evidence limitation reported. |

The next concrete recommendation is **step 1**: settle and prove the single-passage handover on the saved sequence 1207 before extending the prototype into the production runner. The rest of the table is the proposed order, not completed implementation.